Proxy-Control Identifiability Under Intervention-Induced Measurement Distortion

Version 0.4 — Structural Non-Identifiability, Target-Relevant Observability, and Finite-Sample Contraction

Ouadi Maakoul + AI Collaborative Research

---

Abstract

We formalize Proxy-Control Identifiability (PCI) as the problem of identifying a latent causal target when interventions simultaneously modify the latent dynamics and the observation mechanism. We consider partially observed systems

X_{t+1} = F_\theta(X_t, U_t) + \eta_t, \qquad Y_t = h_\phi(X_t, U_{0:t}) + \epsilon_t,

where $X_t$ is latent, $Y_t$ is a proxy, and $U_t$ is an intervention with both therapeutic and diagnostic components. We define intervention-conditioned observational equivalence and a target-pushforward Wasserstein gap $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ measuring worst-case target disagreement among observationally indistinguishable models in an admissible class $\mathcal{Q}$. We prove, by explicit multi-step construction, that identical intervention-conditioned proxy distributions can coexist with arbitrarily different latent target trajectories. We then construct a two-state dynamical counterexample demonstrating that identifying and removing the direct intervention-to-proxy channel is insufficient when target-relevant latent directions remain dynamically unobservable. A conditional recovery theorem is stated, with a rigorous linear-Gaussian specialization and a conditional nonlinear proposition. We derive a finite-sample bound $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \lesssim \sqrt{(q + \log(1/\delta))/(N\lambda_{\min}(I_T))}$, where $I_T$ is the target Fisher information. The framework separates three logically distinct structural requirements — channel separation, dynamic excitation, and target-relevant observability — from the separate statistical requirement of model identification. We state the candidate novelty as a formal problem class, not as a claim of an unprecedented mechanism.

---

1. Introduction

Many control and decision systems act on latent states $X_t \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$ that cannot be measured directly. Decisions rely on observable proxies $Y_t \in \mathcal{Y} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$. When the intervention $U_t \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$ used to modify $X_t$ also modifies the observation mechanism, an observed improvement in $Y_t$ may reflect either a genuine change in $X_t$ or a direct intervention-induced measurement effect.

We study the system

X_{t+1} = F_\theta(X_t, U_t) + \eta_t, \tag{1}

Y_t = h_\phi(X_t, U_{0:t}) + \epsilon_t, \tag{2}

with two conceptually distinct causal pathways:

U_t \to X_{t+1} \to Y_{t+1}, \qquad U_t \to Y_t. \tag{3}

The second pathway creates the central difficulty. We define the problematic regime as $D_U h_\phi \neq 0$, where $D_U h_\phi$ denotes the sensitivity of the observation to the current intervention. We refer to the direct pathway $U \to Y$, unmediated by $X$, as Intervention-Induced Proxy Distortion (IIPD).

For a target functional $T : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$, the naive inference

\Delta Y > 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \Delta T(X) < 0 \tag{4}

is not generally valid. The central question of this work is:

When can an intervention-sensitive proxy identify a latent causal target that the controller cannot observe directly?

We call this problem Proxy-Control Identifiability (PCI).

1.1 Scope and contribution

The framework deliberately separates identification from control. The contributions are:

1. A multi-step structural non-identifiability theorem (Theorem A) showing that identical proxy laws can coexist with different target distributions within an admissible model class.
2. A two-state dynamical counterexample (Theorem C) showing that eliminating the direct intervention-to-proxy channel is insufficient when target-relevant latent modes remain dynamically unobservable.
3. A conditional recovery theorem (Theorem B) under explicit assumptions, with a rigorous linear-Gaussian specialization and a conditional nonlinear proposition.
4. A finite-sample bound on $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ in terms of the target Fisher information.

We do not claim that any individual component (partial observability, persistent excitation, dual control, experimental design) is novel. The candidate novelty is the coupling of direct intervention-to-proxy distortion with target-relative observability into a single formal identifiability problem, together with explicit impossibility and recovery conditions.

