2. Mathematical Model

2.1 Latent dynamics and observation

Let $X_t \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n$, $U_t \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$, $Y_t \in \mathcal{Y} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m$. The latent dynamics are

X_{t+1} = F_\theta(X_t, U_t) + \eta_t, \qquad \eta_t \sim P_{\eta,\theta}, \tag{5}

and the observation model is

Y_t = h_\phi(X_t, U_{0:t}) + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim P_{\epsilon,\phi}. \tag{6}

The dependence on the intervention history $U_{0:t} = (U_0, \ldots, U_t)$, rather than on $U_t$ alone, is required for Theorem A and is natural in settings where cumulative intervention exposure affects measurement.

The Jacobian of the observation function decomposes as

D_X h_\phi \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}, \qquad D_U h_\phi \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times p}, \tag{7}

where $D_U h_\phi$ denotes sensitivity to the current intervention. The problematic regime is $D_U h_\phi \neq 0$.

2.2 Target functional

Let $T : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$ be the scientific target of interest. The framework does not require $T$ to be the full state. When $q < n$, target identification is strictly weaker than full-state observability.

2.3 Candidate model space

A candidate model is

M = (G, \theta, \phi, F_\theta, h_\phi, P_{\eta,\theta}, P_{\epsilon,\phi}), \tag{8}

where $G$ denotes the causal structure (the intervention graph). After observing $\mathcal{D}_t = \{Y_{0:t}, U_{0:t-1}\}$ and given an intervention protocol $U$, the admissible model set is

\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}_t, U) \subseteq \mathcal{M}. \tag{9}

The set $\mathcal{Q}$ may contain uncertainty over $G$, $F$, $h$, $\theta$, $\phi$, and noise distributions.

Standing assumption. All results are stated relative to a fixed admissible model class $\mathcal{Q}$. The gap $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ is model-class-relative: two observationally equivalent models in a broader class may disagree on the target even when they agree in $\mathcal{Q}$. This is intentional: identification claims must specify the admissible model class.

---

3. Intervention-Conditioned Equivalence

3.1 Observational equivalence

Definition 1 (Intervention-Conditioned Observational Equivalence). Two models $M_i, M_j \in \mathcal{M}$ are intervention-conditioned observationally equivalent under $(\mathcal{D}_t, U_{t:T})$, written $M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j$, if

P_{M_i}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) = P_{M_j}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) \tag{10}

as probability measures on $\mathcal{Y}^{T-t+1}$. When densities exist, this is equivalent to

D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(P_{M_i}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) \,\|\, P_{M_j}(Y_{t:T} \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)\right) = 0. \tag{11}

The equivalence is causal in the sense that interventions are $do(U)$-style and the model class includes $G$. Two models with the same predictive law but different causal graphs are distinguished by $G$.

3.2 Target equivalence

Definition 2 (Latent-Target Equivalence). Two models are latent-target equivalent, written $M_i \sim_T M_j$, if

P_{M_i}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) = P_{M_j}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t). \tag{12}

The fundamental identification question is whether

M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j \;\Longrightarrow\; M_i \sim_T M_j. \tag{13}

If (13) fails, the proxy does not uniquely determine the target within the admissible model class.

