4. Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap

4.1 Target-pushforward Wasserstein

Let $T_\# \mu$ denote the pushforward of a state distribution $\mu$ under $T$. Define the target-pushforward Wasserstein-1 distance

W_1^T(\mu, \nu) = W_1(T_\# \mu, T_\# \nu) = \sup_{f \in \mathrm{Lip}_1(\mathbb{R}^q)} \left| \int f \, d(T_\# \mu) - \int f \, d(T_\# \nu) \right|. \tag{14}

The critic class is $\{f \circ T : f \in \mathrm{Lip}_1(\mathbb{R}^q)\}$, a subset of $\mathrm{Lip}_{\mathrm{Lip}(T)}(\mathcal{X})$, so $W_1^T(\mu, \nu) \leq \mathrm{Lip}(T) \, W_1(\mu, \nu)$. Using the pushforward metric rather than the full-state Wasserstein distance makes the gap target-relevant and finite under mild moment conditions.

4.2 Definition

Definition 3 (Proxy-Control Identifiability Gap). The gap over horizon $T$ is

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{D}_t, U_{t:T}) = \sup_{\substack{M_i, M_j \in \mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}_t, U) \\ M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j}} W_1^T\!\left(P_{M_i}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t),\, P_{M_j}(T(X_T) \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t)\right). \tag{15}

4.3 Regularity conditions

For $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ to be finite and to satisfy the identification implications below, we require:

· (C1) Compactness/tightness. $\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}_t, U)$ is compact in a topology making $M \mapsto P_M(T(X_T) \mid U, \mathcal{D}_t)$ continuous.
· (C2) Finite first moments. $\mathbb{E}_{M}[|T(X_T)|] < \infty$ for all $M \in \mathcal{Q}$.
· (C3) Measurable selection. The supremum in (15) is attained, or is replaced by an essential supremum over a $\sigma$-finite measure.

Under (C1)–(C3):

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{target identification within } \mathcal{Q}, \tag{16}

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} > 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{non-identifiability within } \mathcal{Q}. \tag{17}

The gap measures decision-relevant ambiguity, not generic model uncertainty. Two models may disagree strongly about unobserved portions of $X_T$ while agreeing on $T(X_T)$; such disagreement contributes nothing to $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$.

