5. Structural Non-Identifiability

5.1 Theorem A (multi-step non-identifiability)

Theorem A. Consider the scalar system $X_t, Y_t, U_t \in \mathbb{R}$ with $T(X) = X$ and admissible parameter class $\{(k, \alpha, \beta, \sigma^2) : \alpha \in [\alpha_{\min}, \alpha_{\max}], \beta \in \mathbb{R}, k \neq 0, \sigma^2 > 0\}$. Let

X_{t+1} = X_t - \alpha U_t, \tag{18}

Y_t = k X_t + \beta U_t + k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2) \text{ i.i.d.} \tag{19}

Then for any horizon $T \geq 1$, any intervention sequence $\{U_t\}_{t=0}^{T-1}$, and any admissible $\alpha_1, \alpha_2$,

M(k, \alpha_1, \beta, \sigma^2) \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M(k, \alpha_2, \beta, \sigma^2), \tag{20}

while

P_{M(k, \alpha_i)}(T(X_T) \mid U) = \delta_{X_0 - \alpha_i \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s}. \tag{21}

Consequently,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \geq |\alpha_1 - \alpha_2| \cdot \left| \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s \right|. \tag{22}

Proof. Iterating (18):

X_t = X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s. \tag{23}

Substituting into (19):

Y_t = k\left(X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s\right) + \beta U_t + k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \epsilon_t = k X_0 + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t. \tag{24}

The $\alpha$-dependence cancels exactly at every $t$. Therefore

P_{M(k, \alpha)}(Y_{0:T-1} \mid U_{0:T-1}) = \mathcal{N}\!\left(k X_0 \mathbf{1} + \beta U_{0:T-1}, \sigma^2 I_T\right), \tag{25}

independent of $\alpha$. Hence (20) holds. Since both target distributions are Dirac, $W_1^T = |\alpha_1 - \alpha_2| \cdot |\sum_{s} U_s|$, giving (22). $\blacksquare$

5.2 Interpretation

Theorem A establishes the structural possibility:

\text{identical proxy law} \;\not\Rightarrow\; \text{identical latent target}. \tag{26}

The direct channel $U \to Y$ can exactly compensate for a genuine intervention-mediated change $U \to X \to Y$. The proxy trajectory remains unchanged while the latent state changes.

Remark 1 (Scope of unboundedness). If $\alpha$ is restricted to a bounded interval and $U$ to a bounded set, then $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ is bounded. Unbounded gaps require unbounded admissible model or intervention classes. The rigorous statement is: direct intervention-induced observation distortion can produce arbitrarily large target ambiguity when the admissible model and intervention classes permit arbitrarily large compensating latent dynamics.

Remark 2 (Necessity of history dependence). The construction requires $h_\phi$ to depend on $U_{0:t}$, not on $U_t$ alone. At $t = 2$, if $h_\phi$ depends only on $U_t$, the direct channel cannot cancel the cumulative latent effect $\alpha(U_0 + U_1)$ without an additional term in $U_0$. This is a genuine restriction of the theorem, not an artifact.

