6. Channel Separation Versus Channel Estimation

6.1 Dual-role intervention

Write

U_t = U_t^{\mathrm{ctrl}} + \delta_t v, \tag{27}

where $U_t^{\mathrm{ctrl}}$ is the therapeutic component and $\delta_t v$ is a diagnostic perturbation.

6.2 Diagnostic ratio

For $v \in T_U \mathcal{U}$ with $|v| = 1$, define

\rho(v; X, U) = \frac{|D_U h_\phi(X, U) \, v|_{\mathcal{Y}}}{|D_U F_\theta(X, U) \, v|_{\mathcal{X}} + \lambda}, \qquad \lambda > 0, \tag{28}

and $\rho^\star(X, U) = \sup_{|v| \leq 1} \rho(v; X, U)$. A direction $v^\star$ is $\rho_{\mathrm{crit}}$-diagnostic if $\rho(v^\star; X, U) \geq \rho_{\mathrm{crit}}$.

6.3 Separation is not estimation

Proposition 1. $\rho(v^\star) \geq \rho_{\mathrm{crit}} > 0$ does not imply that $D_U h_\phi$ is consistently estimable from finite data.

Proof sketch. Estimation of $D_U h_\phi$ requires (i) sufficient variation in $U_t$ (persistent excitation of the input), (ii) bounded observation noise, and (iii) separation of the direct channel from the latent response in the observation equation. The ratio $\rho$ controls only the relative magnitude of the two sensitivities at a point; it does not control their statistical identifiability. A direction with large $\rho$ may still have small absolute sensitivity $|D_U h_\phi v|$ relative to noise, so the signal-to-noise ratio can be insufficient for consistent estimation. $\blacksquare$

The distinction between channel separation (a structural property of the Jacobians) and channel estimation (a statistical property of the estimator) is essential. The structural conditions below use $\rho$ as a geometric criterion; the statistical conditions use an explicit estimator assumption.

