7. Dynamically Induced Observability and a Pathological Counterexample

7.1 The two-state system

Consider $X_t = (x_{1,t}, x_{2,t})^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$ with dynamics

x_{1,t+1} = x_{1,t} + u_{1,t}, \qquad x_{2,t+1} = x_{2,t} + u_{2,t}, \tag{29}

observation

Y_t = x_{1,t} - x_{2,t} + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t, \tag{30}

and target

T(X) = x_1 + x_2. \tag{31}

The instantaneous observation differential is $dh = (1, -1)$, so

\ker(dh) = \mathrm{span}\{(1, 1)^\top\}. \tag{32}

The target gradient is $dT = (1, 1)$, which lies entirely in the unobservable direction.

7.2 Invariance of the unobservable subspace

The linear dynamics are $F(X, U) = X + U$, with linearization $D_X F = I$. The unobservable direction $(1, 1)^\top$ is invariant under $D_X F$:

D_X F \cdot (1, 1)^\top = (1, 1)^\top. \tag{33}

Consequently, the observable codistribution at any order is

\mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{(1, -1)^\top\}. \tag{34}

7.3 Theorem C (channel separation does not imply target identifiability)

Theorem C. In the system (29)–(31), for every $K \geq 1$ and every $v \in T_U \mathcal{U}$, the direction $(1, 1)^\top$ remains unobservable:

(1, 1)^\top \in \ker \mathcal{O}_K(v). \tag{35}

Consequently,

\ker \mathcal{O}_K(v) \not\subseteq \ker dT \tag{36}

for every admissible diagnostic direction $v$. Therefore no diagnostic intervention can identify $T(X) = x_1 + x_2$ through $Y$, even when $D_U h$ is estimated exactly.

Proof. Let $\delta X = (a, a)^\top$ for some $a \neq 0$. Since $D_X F = I$, we have $D_X F \delta X = \delta X$, so the perturbation is preserved by the dynamics. At every step,

dh \cdot \delta X = (1, -1) \cdot (a, a)^\top = 0. \tag{37}

Therefore $dh \cdot (D_X F)^k \delta X = 0$ for all $k \geq 0$, so $\delta X \in \ker \mathcal{O}_K(v)$. Since $dT \cdot \delta X = (1, 1) \cdot (a, a)^\top = 2a \neq 0$, the inclusion fails. $\blacksquare$

7.4 Interpretation

The direct channel can be perfectly identified ($\beta$ is estimable from the fast response), and $x_1 - x_2$ is observable. But $x_1 + x_2$ is never observable, regardless of the diagnostic input. This shows:

\text{diagnostic channel separation} \;\not\Rightarrow\; \text{target identifiability}. \tag{38}

The counterexample survives perfect estimation of $D_U h_\phi$; it is structural, not statistical.

