8. Target-Relevant Observability

8.1 Nonlinear observability codistribution

For a continuous-time system $\dot{X} = F(X, U)$, $Y = h(X, U)$, the nonlinear observability codistribution is generated by

\mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{dh, dL_F h, dL_F^2 h, \ldots\}. \tag{39}

For discrete-time systems, the analogous object is generated by $\{d(h \circ F^k)\}_{k \geq 0}$.

8.2 Definition

Definition 4 (Local Target Observability). A differentiable target $T : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$ is locally observable through the output system if

\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) \subseteq \ker dT. \tag{40}

For full-state identification, $\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) = \{0\}$. Target observability is strictly weaker whenever $q < n$.

8.3 Linear specialization

For the linear system $X_{t+1} = AX_t + BU_t$, $Y_t = CX_t + DU_t$, with $T(X) = LX$, the finite-horizon observability matrix is

\mathcal{O}_K = \begin{bmatrix} C \\ CA \\ \vdots \\ CA^{K-1} \end{bmatrix}, \tag{41}

and the target-observability condition becomes

\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker L. \tag{42}

This is the clean finite-dimensional condition. Equivalently, there exists $M \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times mK}$ with

L = M \mathcal{O}_K. \tag{43}

The matrix $M$ expresses the target as a linear function of the observable output history.

---

9. Intervention-Induced Dynamic Excitation

9.1 Variational dynamics

Consider the nonlinear variational system

\delta \dot{X} = A(X, U) \delta X + B(X, U) \delta U, \tag{44}

where $A = D_X F$ and $B = D_U F$. A diagnostic perturbation $\delta U_t = \delta_t v$ induces a state perturbation through $Bv$, which then propagates through $A$.

9.2 Definition (intervention-induced observability matrix)

Definition 5. For a linear system $(A, B, C)$, define

\mathscr{O}_K(v) = \begin{bmatrix} CBv \\ CABv \\ \vdots \\ CA^{K-1}Bv \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{mK}. \tag{45}

For nonlinear systems, $\mathscr{O}_K(v)$ denotes the Jacobian of the output trajectory $\{Y_{t+k}\}_{k=0}^{K-1}$ with respect to the diagnostic perturbation $\delta_t v$, evaluated through the variational dynamics (44).

9.3 Target-relevant excitation condition

For a linear target $T(X) = LX$, the target-relevant excitation condition is

\ker \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \subseteq \ker L. \tag{46}

This is the joint requirement that the diagnostic perturbation excites the target-relevant directions and that these directions are observable through the output. It is stronger than either condition alone.

Remark 3. For vector targets, (46) is the correct condition. Scalar rank inequalities such as $\mathrm{rank}(\mathscr{O}_K) \geq \mathrm{rank}(L)$ are necessary but not sufficient: they do not ensure that the observable directions contain the target-relevant ones.

