10. Persistent Excitation

10.1 Definition

Let $\{\Phi_t\}_{t=0}^{N-1} \subset \mathbb{R}^{d \times p}$ be a regressor sequence. It is persistently exciting of order $K$ if there exists $c > 0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $N$,

\sum_{t=0}^{N-1} \Phi_t \Phi_t^\top \succeq c I_d. \tag{47}

10.2 Induced regressors

For the diagnostic experiment with perturbation $\delta U_t = \delta_t v^\star$, the induced regressor is

\Phi_t = \mathscr{O}_K(v^\star) \delta_t. \tag{48}

The PE condition (47) applies to $\Phi_t$, not to $\delta_t$ alone.

10.3 PE is not consistency

Proposition 2. Persistent excitation of $\{\Phi_t\}$ is a necessary condition for consistent estimation of the target-relevant parameters, but it is not sufficient. Consistency additionally requires model-class correctness, estimator regularity, and bounded noise (or appropriate moment conditions).

Proof sketch. PE ensures that the normal equations $\sum_t \Phi_t \Phi_t^\top \hat{\theta} = \sum_t \Phi_t y_t$ have a unique solution. But if the true data-generating process is not in $\mathcal{Q}$, or if the estimator is biased, PE does not ensure that $\hat{\theta} \to \theta^\star$. $\blacksquare$

The theorem below therefore treats PE and consistency as logically separate assumptions. Conflating them would hide an entire system-identification theorem inside the phrase "PE."

