11. Conditional Recovery

11.1 Separation of structural and statistical claims

The structural results (Sections 5–9) establish when target ambiguity can exist and what geometric conditions are necessary to remove it. Statistical recovery requires a separate assumption:

\mathcal{Q}_N \to \mathcal{Q}^\star \quad \text{in an appropriate topology.} \tag{49}

This is an estimation statement, not an observability statement.

11.2 Theorem B (conditional target-identifiability recovery)

Theorem B. Let $\mathcal{Q}_N \subseteq \mathcal{M}$ be the model set compatible with $N$ observations and diagnostic interventions. Assume:

· (A1) Correct specification. The true data-generating process satisfies $M^\star \in \mathcal{Q}$.
· (A2) Regularity. $M \mapsto P_M(T(X_T) \mid U, \mathcal{D}_t)$ is continuous under the topology on $\mathcal{Q}$.
· (A3) Compactness/tightness. $\mathcal{Q}$ is compact or tight with uniformly bounded first moments of $T(X_T)$.
· (A4) Channel identifiability. There exists a consistent estimator $\widehat{D_U h}_N \to D_U h$ in probability under the diagnostic protocol.
· (A5) Target-relevant observability. For the true system, $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker dT$.
· (A6) Informative excitation. The diagnostic intervention produces PE regressors $\Phi_t$ in the sense of (47).
· (A7) Consistent model-set contraction. $\mathcal{Q}_N \to [M^\star]_{\mathcal{D},U}$ in probability, in set-valued topology.
· (A8) Uniform target continuity. $M \mapsto P_M(T(X_T) \mid U, \mathcal{D}_t)$ is uniformly continuous on $\mathcal{Q}$.

Then

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{D}_t \cup \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{diag}}^N, U \cup U_{\mathrm{diag}}^N) \xrightarrow[N \to \infty]{P} 0. \tag{50}

Proof. By (A7), with probability tending to one, $\mathcal{Q}_N$ contracts to $[M^\star]_{\mathcal{D},U}$. By (A5) and (A6), every model in the limiting equivalence class that remains after consistent channel estimation (A4) is target-equivalent to $M^\star$. Therefore

P_M(T(X_T) \mid U, \mathcal{D}_t) = P_{M^\star}(T(X_T) \mid U, \mathcal{D}_t) \tag{51}

for all $M$ in the limiting class. By (A8), the target-distribution map is uniformly continuous, so its image under $\mathcal{Q}_N$ has diameter tending to zero. Since $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ is the worst-case Wasserstein diameter of this image over observationally indistinguishable models, $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0$ in probability. $\blacksquare$

11.3 Interpretation

Theorem B does not claim that the structural conditions alone imply statistical recovery. Instead, the logical architecture is:

\text{channel separation} + \text{dynamic excitation} + \text{target-relevant observability} + \text{consistent estimation} \;\Rightarrow\; \Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0. \tag{52}

The first three terms are structural; the fourth is statistical. This prevents the theorem from silently importing a nonlinear system-identification result.

