12. Linear-Gaussian Rigorous Specialization

12.1 Known dynamics, unknown direct channel

Consider

X_{t+1} = AX_t + BU_t + \eta_t, \qquad \eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\eta), \tag{53}

Y_t = CX_t + DU_t + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\epsilon), \tag{54}

with $T(X) = LX$ and $A, B, C, \Sigma_\eta, \Sigma_\epsilon$ known. Only $D$ is unknown.

Theorem B-LG. Under (A5), the condition $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker L$ implies $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0$ as the diagnostic experiment identifies $D$ consistently.

Proof. Under (A5), there exists $M \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times mK}$ with $L = M \mathcal{O}_K$. The target decomposes as

LX_T = M \mathcal{O}_K X_T = M \begin{bmatrix} C X_T \\ CA X_T \\ \vdots \\ CA^{K-1} X_T \end{bmatrix}. \tag{55}

Each block $CA^k X_T$ is expressible through the observation recursion

CA^k X_T = Y_{T-k} - DU_{T-k} - \epsilon_{T-k} - \sum_{j=1}^{k-1} CA^{k-1-j} B U_{T-k+j} - (\text{noise terms}). \tag{56}

The cleaned observation $\widetilde{Y}_t = Y_t - \widehat{D}_N U_t$ converges in probability to $CX_t + \epsilon_t$ by consistency of $\widehat{D}_N$. Since the target depends on $\{Y_t\}$ only through the cleaned observations and known inputs, any two models producing the same cleaned-observation law must produce the same target law. Hence $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0$. $\blacksquare$

12.2 Unknown dynamic parameters

When $A$, $B$, $C$ are also unknown, the previous argument is insufficient. Consistency of $\widehat{D}_N$ no longer suffices, because the observability matrix $\mathcal{O}_K$ itself depends on the unknown parameters. A separate identifiability condition on $(A, B, C)$ is required:

\vartheta = \mathrm{vec}(A, B, C, D) \quad \text{identifiable from } \{Y_{0:N}, U_{0:N}\}. \tag{57}

Sufficient statistical conditions include a non-degenerate Fisher information matrix:

\frac{1}{N} \mathcal{I}_N(\vartheta) \succeq c I \quad \text{asymptotically, for some } c > 0. \tag{58}

Without such a condition, the model set $\mathcal{Q}_N$ may not contract to a singleton, and the target gap may not vanish.

12.3 Nonlinear case

For general nonlinear systems, the previous argument does not extend without uniform observability over the admissible compact set. We state the result as a conditional proposition.

Proposition 3 (Conditional nonlinear recovery). Assume (A1)–(A8), and additionally:

· (N1) Uniform observability. The observability codistribution $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$ has constant rank on the admissible compact set $K \subseteq \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U}$.
· (N2) Uniform target observability. $\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) \subseteq \ker dT$ uniformly on $K$.
· (N3) Uniform PE. The induced regressors $\Phi_t$ satisfy (47) uniformly over $K$.

Then $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0$ in probability under the same conclusion as Theorem B.

Status. The proposition is stated as a target for rigorous proof, not as a theorem. A complete proof requires a nonlinear observability-identifiability rank condition and a uniform consistency argument that we have not developed here.

---

13. Finite-Sample Bound on $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$

The asymptotic results above do not specify how quickly $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ contracts at finite $N$. Below we derive an explicit bound.

13.1 Setup

Fix a parametric model class $\Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ with $M_\vartheta$ indexed by $\vartheta$. Let $\mathcal{D}_N = (Y_{0:N}, U_{0:N})$ be the observed trajectory under a fixed intervention protocol. The log-likelihood is $\ell_N(\vartheta)$, the MLE is $\hat{\vartheta}_N = \arg\max_{\vartheta \in \Theta} \ell_N(\vartheta)$, and the per-observation Fisher information is

I_1(\vartheta) = \mathbb{E}_{M_\vartheta}\!\left[-\nabla^2_\vartheta \log p_\vartheta(Y_1 \mid U_0)\right] \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}. \tag{59}

13.2 Confidence set

Define the likelihood-ratio confidence set at level $1 - \delta$:

\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta) = \left\{\vartheta \in \Theta : 2\left(\ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) - \ell_N(\vartheta)\right) \leq \chi^2_d(1-\delta)\right\}. \tag{60}

Under standard regularity, Wilks' theorem gives $P_{M_{\vartheta^\star}}(\vartheta^\star \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \to 1 - \delta$. A second-order Taylor expansion at $\hat{\vartheta}_N$ gives, for $\vartheta \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$,

(\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N)^\top J_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N)(\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N) \leq \chi^2_d(1-\delta) + o_P(1), \tag{61}

where $J_N$ is the observed Fisher information. Since $J_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N)/N \to I_1(\vartheta^\star)$ in probability,

\|\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N\|_{I_1} \leq \sqrt{\frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N}} + o_P(1) \quad \text{uniformly for } \vartheta \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta). \tag{62}

13.3 Target Lipschitz constant

Define the target map

g : \Theta \to \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^q), \qquad g(\vartheta) = P_{M_\vartheta}(T(X_T) \mid U, \mathcal{D}_t). \tag{63}

Assume $g$ is Lipschitz with respect to the Fisher metric on $\Theta$ and the target-pushforward Wasserstein-1 metric on $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^q)$:

W_1^T(g(\vartheta_1), g(\vartheta_2)) \leq L_T \|\vartheta_1 - \vartheta_2\|_{I_1}, \qquad \forall \vartheta_1, \vartheta_2 \in \Theta. \tag{64}

Key observation. $L_T$ is finite precisely when the target-relevant observability condition (A5) holds. If the target direction is dynamically unobservable, $L_T = \infty$ and the bound below is vacuous. This is the correct behavior: the finite-sample bound is contingent on the structural condition.

13.4 Main bound

For any $\vartheta_1, \vartheta_2 \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$,

W_1^T(g(\vartheta_1), g(\vartheta_2)) \leq L_T \|\vartheta_1 - \vartheta_2\|_{I_1} \leq 2 L_T \sqrt{\frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N}}. \tag{65}

Taking the supremum over observationally indistinguishable pairs within $\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$:

\boxed{\;\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \leq 2 L_T \sqrt{\frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N \,\lambda_{\min}(I_1)}}.\;} \tag{66}

Using the standard asymptotic expansion $\chi^2_d(1-\delta) \approx d + 2\sqrt{d \log(1/\delta)} + 2\log(1/\delta)$,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \lesssim L_T \sqrt{\frac{d + \log(1/\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_1)}}. \tag{67}

13.5 Target-relevant refinement

The bound (67) scales with the full parameter dimension $d$. But the target map $g$ depends only on the target-relevant directions. Define the target information matrix

I_T(\vartheta) = \left[\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial \vartheta}\right) I_1(\vartheta)^{-1} \left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial \vartheta}\right)^\top\right]^{-1} \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times q}. \tag{68}

Under target-relevant observability, $I_T$ is positive definite. A refined bound in terms of the target information is

\boxed{\;\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \lesssim \sqrt{\frac{q + \log(1/\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_T)}}.\;} \tag{69}

The dimensional penalty is now $q$, not $d$. This is the quantitative content of target observability being weaker than full-state observability: when $q \ll d$, the finite-sample cost is correspondingly smaller.

13.6 Nonparametric extension

If $\Theta$ is a Hölder ball of smoothness $\beta$ in dimension $d$ with metric entropy $\log \mathcal{N}(\epsilon, \Theta, \|\cdot\|) \lesssim \epsilon^{-d/\beta}$, the confidence-set radius becomes

r_N(\delta) \asymp \left(\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{N}\right)^{\frac{\beta}{2\beta + d}}, \tag{70}

and

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \lesssim L_T \left(\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{N}\right)^{\frac{\beta}{2\beta + d}}. \tag{71}

The parametric rate $\sqrt{d/N}$ is recovered as $\beta \to \infty$.

13.7 How the structural conditions enter the bound

The bound (69) makes the logical architecture explicit:

Structural condition Role in the bound
Channel separation $\rho(v^\star) \geq \rho_{\mathrm{crit}}$ Ensures $I_1$ is non-singular in the $D_U h$ direction, so the direct channel is estimable.
Target-relevant observability $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker dT$ Ensures $L_T < \infty$; otherwise the bound is vacuous.
Dynamic excitation (persistent) Ensures $\lambda_{\min}(I_T) > 0$; otherwise the rate degenerates.
Consistent inference (A6–A8) Ensures the confidence set is well-calibrated.

13.8 Caveats

1. Locality. $L_T$ and $I_T$ are defined pointwise; uniform control over $\Theta$ requires uniform versions of (64) and positive-definiteness of $I_T$.
2. Correct specification. If $M^\star \notin \mathcal{Q}$, the bound is irrelevant.
3. Loose constants. The factor of 2 and the $\chi^2$ approximation are not tight. Sharp constants require a Bernstein–von Mises refinement.

