14. Multi-Time-Scale Channel Separation

When $\tau_Y \ll \tau_X$, with $\varepsilon_\tau = \tau_Y / \tau_X \ll 1$, a local expansion takes the form

\Delta Y = D_X h \Delta X + D_U h \Delta U + O(\varepsilon_\tau^2) + \epsilon. \tag{72}

This motivates a two-stage experimental strategy:

1. High-frequency diagnostic perturbations isolate the direct channel $\Delta Y^{\mathrm{direct}} = D_U h \Delta U$.
2. Slow latent response isolates the state channel $\Delta Y^{\mathrm{state}} = D_X h \Delta X$.

The condition $\tau_Y \ll \tau_X$ is not sufficient by itself: it must be verified experimentally and accompanied by an identifiable signal model.

---

15. Diagnostic Intervention Design

The design problem is not simply $\max_v \rho(v)$. It is a constrained information-design problem:

\begin{aligned}
\max_{v, \delta_{0:N-1}} \quad & \lambda_{\min}(I_T(v, \delta_{0:N-1})) \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & |D_U F \, v| \leq \varepsilon_F, \\
& |D_U h \, v| \geq \varepsilon_h, \\
& \ker \mathscr{O}_K(v) \subseteq \ker dT, \\
& \{\Phi_t\} \text{ satisfies PE}, \\
& U_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}.
\end{aligned} \tag{73}

Maximizing the smallest eigenvalue of the target information matrix is equivalent to minimizing the asymptotic variance of the target estimate in the worst direction. This is a concrete, solvable experimental-design problem.

---

16. Identifiability Versus Control

The theory does not require identification and control to occur in separate time intervals. A dual-control policy may combine $U_t^{\mathrm{ctrl}}$ and $U_t^{\mathrm{diag}}$. The distinction is informational:

· Control asks: which intervention optimizes the target given the current belief?
· Identification asks: which observations and interventions distinguish models that disagree about the target?

The two objectives may be optimized jointly. The architecture is

\text{diagnostic information} + \text{therapeutic action} \to \text{belief update} \to \text{target-aware control}. \tag{74}

---

17. Safety as a Separate Layer

Safety should not be folded into the identifiability theorem. Suppose the controller must satisfy

P(X_t \in \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{safe}}) \geq 1 - \delta. \tag{75}

Under a Gaussian belief $X_t \sim \mathcal{N}(\hat{X}_t, \Sigma_t)$, the confidence ellipsoid is

\mathcal{K}_\delta = \{x : (x - \hat{X}_t)^\top \Sigma_t^{-1} (x - \hat{X}_t) \leq \chi^2_n(1-\delta)\}. \tag{76}

A sufficient robust condition is $h_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) \geq 0$ for all $x \in \mathcal{K}_\delta$. This is a safety condition on the controller, not an identifiability condition. The architecture is layered:

\text{PCI layer} \to \text{state/target inference} \to \text{safety layer} \to \text{control layer}. \tag{77}

The Gaussian ellipsoid should not be interpreted as a universal representation of a non-Gaussian posterior.

---

18. Falsification Program

F1. Observational compensation. Theorem A provides one such construction.

F2. Diagnostic-only excitation. Theorem C provides one such construction.

F3. Hidden symmetry. Construct $g : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X}$ with $h(g(X), U) = h(X, U)$ while $T(g(X)) \neq T(X)$.

F4. Excitation failure. Construct a diagnostic sequence satisfying a local rank condition at one point but failing PE over the horizon.

F5. Model-class misspecification. Construct $M^\star \notin \mathcal{Q}$; then $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$-contraction within $\mathcal{Q}$ does not imply identification of $M^\star$.

F6. Noise-limited contraction. Determine whether $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ can be reduced below a scientifically meaningful threshold at finite $N$, not merely asymptotically.

F7. Intervention-induced nonstationarity. Test whether the diagnostic intervention changes the observation mechanism itself ($h_\phi \to h_{\phi(U)}$). If the channel being estimated evolves during the diagnostic experiment, a static $D_U h$ model is inadequate.

---

19. Gate 1 Decision Criterion

Gate 1.1 — Structural ambiguity. Find admissible $M_1, M_2$ with $M_1 \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_2$ but $M_1 \not\sim_T M_2$. If none exist, PCI is unnecessary for that model class.

Gate 1.2 — Channel separability. Determine whether $D_U h$ can be statistically separated from the latent response. If not, target identification fails.

Gate 1.3 — Dynamic excitation. Determine whether admissible interventions can excite latent modes relevant to $T$. If $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \not\subseteq \ker dT$ for every admissible experiment, target identification is structurally impossible.

Gate 1.4 — Statistical identification. Determine whether the unknown model components are statistically identifiable. PE is a candidate condition, not a substitute.

Gate 1.5 — Target-gap contraction. Establish either $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0$ asymptotically, or a finite-sample bound $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \leq \varepsilon_N$ with $\varepsilon_N \to 0$.

---

20. Central Structural Result

The theory is summarized by:

D_U h \neq 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{proxy distortion} \;\Rightarrow\; \text{possible target ambiguity} \;\Rightarrow\; \Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} > 0. \tag{78}

The remedy requires:

\text{channel separation} + \text{dynamic excitation} + \ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker dT + \text{statistical identification} \;\Rightarrow\; \Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0. \tag{79}

The central mathematical object is the coupled tuple

\boxed{(D_U h, \; D_U F, \; \mathcal{O}_F(h), \; dT, \; \mathcal{Q}).} \tag{80}

---

21. Theoretical Novelty

The framework does not claim novelty for any of the following in isolation: partial observability, nonlinear observability, persistent excitation, dual control, experimental design, causal intervention, or belief-space control. The candidate novelty is the explicit formulation of a target-specific identifiability problem in which the intervention simultaneously acts on the latent dynamics and the observation mechanism, together with:

· a target-pushforward ambiguity metric $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$;
· a constructive multi-step non-identifiability result (Theorem A);
· a structural counterexample separating channel estimation from target identification (Theorem C);
· an intervention-induced dynamic excitation condition and a target-relative observability criterion;
· a conditional contraction theorem with a finite-sample bound.

The scientific claim remains:

\boxed{\text{PCI is a proposed formal problem class whose novelty must be established by prior-art separation and theorem-level consequences.}} \tag{81}

---

22. Open Mathematical Problems

P1. Quantitative lower bound on $\lambda_{\min}(I_T)$. Express $\lambda_{\min}(I_T)$ explicitly in terms of $\rho(v^\star)$, the observability rank, and the input covariance. A natural conjecture is

\lambda_{\min}(I_T) \succeq c(\rho^\star, \mathrm{rank}\,\mathcal{O}_K) \cdot \mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{N-1} \Phi_t \Phi_t^\top\right], \tag{82}

but this requires proof.

P2. Uniform Lipschitz constant. Prove $L_T < \infty$ uniformly over a compact admissible set under the constant-rank condition on $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$.

P3. Misspecification bound. Replace (A1) with a bound on the misspecification bias.

P4. Optimal diagnostic intervention. Solve (73) with explicit dependence on the structural parameters.

P5. Adaptive diagnostic control. Allow $v_t$ to depend on the evolving belief.

P6. Nonstationary measurement mechanism. Allow $h_{\phi_t}$ to evolve during the intervention.

P7. Distributionally robust PCI. Replace parametric uncertainty with ambiguity sets over transition and observation kernels.

---

23. Conclusion

Proxy-Control Identifiability formalizes a fundamental failure mode of proxy-mediated intervention systems:

\boxed{\text{an intervention can improve the measurement without improving the latent target.}} \tag{83}

The framework begins with $D_U h \neq 0$, which creates a direct intervention-to-proxy pathway. We define the target-specific ambiguity measure $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ and prove constructively that

M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j \;\not\Rightarrow\; M_i \sim_T M_j. \tag{84}

The two-state counterexample (Theorem C) establishes that identifying the direct measurement channel is insufficient when the target-sensitive latent modes remain dynamically unobservable.

The resulting decomposition is

\text{PCI} = \text{channel separation} + \text{dynamic excitation} + \text{target-relevant observability} + \text{statistical identification}. \tag{85}

The first three terms are structural; the fourth is statistical. The finite-sample bound

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \lesssim \sqrt{\frac{q + \log(1/\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_T)}} \tag{86}

quantifies the contraction rate in terms of the target information, and the structural conditions enter the bound through their effect on $L_T$ and $\lambda_{\min}(I_T)$.

The impossibility criterion is

\forall U_{\mathrm{diag}} \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{diag}}, \; \exists M_i, M_j : M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j, \; M_i \not\sim_T M_j, \tag{87}

which implies no admissible intervention can identify the target. Conversely, sufficient recovery requires:

\begin{aligned}
& \text{direct proxy channel identifiable}, \\
& \text{target-relevant latent modes excited}, \\
& \ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker dT, \\
& \text{model parameters statistically identifiable}, \\
& \text{inferred model set contracts consistently}.
\end{aligned} \tag{88}

Under these conditions, $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0$.

The central theoretical question is

\boxed{\textbf{When can an intervention-sensitive proxy causally identify a latent target that cannot be observed directly?}} \tag{89}

The most important next mathematical task is P1: an explicit lower bound on $\lambda_{\min}(I_T)$ in terms of $\rho(v^\star)$ and the observability rank. That bound would connect the structural conditions to the finite-sample rate and turn the conditional Theorem B into a theorem with measurable consequences.

