Appendix A — Measure-Theoretic Foundations of the Equivalence Relation

A.1 Canonical probability space

Fix a probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$ rich enough to carry:

· the latent trajectory $\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ on $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X}))$,
· the process noise $\{\eta_t\}_{t \geq 0}$,
· the observation noise $\{\epsilon_t\}_{t \geq 0}$,
· the intervention sequence $\{U_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ on $(\mathcal{U}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{U}))$.

For a candidate model $M = (G, \theta, \phi, F_\theta, h_\phi, P_{\eta,\theta}, P_{\epsilon,\phi})$, the joint law of $(X_{0:T}, Y_{0:T}, U_{0:T})$ is uniquely determined by the recursive kernels

X_{t+1} \mid (X_t, U_t) \sim \mathcal{K}_{\theta}(X_t, U_t, \cdot), \qquad \mathcal{K}_\theta(x, u, A) = P_{\eta,\theta}(A - F_\theta(x, u)), \tag{A.1}

Y_t \mid (X_t, U_{0:t}) \sim \mathcal{L}_{\phi}(X_t, U_{0:t}, \cdot), \qquad \mathcal{L}_\phi(x, u_{0:t}, B) = P_{\epsilon,\phi}(B - h_\phi(x, u_{0:t})). \tag{A.2}

Regularity assumption (R0). For every $M \in \mathcal{M}$:

1. $\mathcal{K}_\theta : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X}) \to [0,1]$ is a Borel-measurable Markov kernel.
2. $\mathcal{L}_\phi : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U}^{t+1} \times \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{Y}) \to [0,1]$ is a Borel-measurable Markov kernel.
3. The initial state $X_0$ has a law $\mu_0$ that is common to all models in $\mathcal{M}$ (or the initial law is part of $M$ and the equivalence relation includes it).

Under (R0), the Ionescu-Tulcea theorem guarantees existence and uniqueness of the joint law

\mathbb{P}_M(dX_{0:T}, dY_{0:T}, dU_{0:T}) = \mu_0(dX_0) \prod_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathcal{K}_\theta(X_t, U_t, dX_{t+1}) \prod_{t=0}^{T} \mathcal{L}_\phi(X_t, U_{0:t}, dY_t) \cdot Q(dU_{0:T}), \tag{A.3}

where $Q$ is the intervention design law (either deterministic or stochastic). When the intervention sequence is deterministic, $Q = \delta_{U_{0:T}}$ and the resulting measure is written $\mathbb{P}_M^{U}$.

A.2 Regular conditional probability

For the equivalence in Definition 1, we need the regular conditional law of $Y_{t:T}$ given $\mathcal{D}_t$ and $U_{t:T}$. Existence is guaranteed by (R0) and standard disintegration (Kallenberg, Foundations of Modern Probability, Ch. 8). Write

P_M(Y_{t:T} \in \cdot \mid U_{t:T}, \mathcal{D}_t) = \mathbb{P}_M^{U}\!\left(Y_{t:T} \in \cdot \mid \sigma(\mathcal{D}_t, U_{t:T})\right). \tag{A.4}

Since $U_{t:T}$ is deterministic in our setting, the conditioning is on $\mathcal{D}_t$ only.

A.3 Consistency of the equivalence relation

Lemma A.1. $\sim_{\mathcal{D},U}$ is an equivalence relation on $\mathcal{M}$ under (R0).

Proof. Reflexivity and symmetry are immediate from equality of measures. Transitivity: if $P_{M_1} = P_{M_2}$ and $P_{M_2} = P_{M_3}$ as measures on $(\mathcal{Y}^{T-t+1}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{Y}^{T-t+1}))$, then $P_{M_1} = P_{M_3}$. $\blacksquare$

Lemma A.2. If two models $M_i, M_j$ satisfy $M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j$ for all horizons $T \geq t$ and all intervention sequences in a class $\mathcal{U}^\ast$, then they induce the same joint law on the observable process $(Y_{0:\infty}, U_{0:\infty})$.

Proof. Equality of finite-dimensional marginals for all $T$ implies equality of the projective limit by the Kolmogorov extension theorem, provided the marginals satisfy the consistency conditions of the Daniell–Kolmogorov theorem. These are automatic from (R0). $\blacksquare$

A.4 Absolute continuity and KL divergence

Lemma A.3. Suppose $M_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} M_j$ and both predictive laws have densities $p_i, p_j$ with respect to a common dominating measure $\nu$. Then

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_i \,\|\, p_j) = 0 \;\Longleftrightarrow\; p_i = p_j \;\; \nu\text{-a.e.} \tag{A.5}

Proof. KL divergence is non-negative and vanishes iff the measures coincide on the $\sigma$-algebra. Under mutual absolute continuity ($p_i \ll p_j$ and $p_j \ll p_i$), equality of densities follows. If the measures are not mutually absolutely continuous, equality of measures still implies $p_i = p_j$ a.e. $\blacksquare$

The KL characterization is therefore a convenient equivalent form of the equivalence relation, but the primitive notion is equality of measures.

