Appendix B — Full Proof of Theorem A and the Necessity of History Dependence

B.1 Full proof of Theorem A

Restatement. Consider $X_t, Y_t, U_t \in \mathbb{R}$, $T(X) = X$, with admissible parameter class $\Theta = \{(k, \alpha, \beta, \sigma^2) : \alpha \in [\alpha_{\min}, \alpha_{\max}], \beta \in \mathbb{R}, k \neq 0, \sigma^2 > 0\}$. The model $M(k, \alpha, \beta, \sigma^2)$ is defined by

X_{t+1} = X_t - \alpha U_t, \tag{B.1}

Y_t = k X_t + \beta U_t + k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2) \text{ i.i.d.} \tag{B.2}

Step 1 (latent trajectory). Iterating (B.1) from the common deterministic initial condition $X_0$:

X_t = X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s, \qquad \forall t \geq 0. \tag{B.3}

The recursion is exact because the dynamics are deterministic given $\{U_t\}$ and $X_0$.

Step 2 (observation law). Substitute (B.3) into (B.2):

\begin{aligned}
Y_t &= k\left(X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s\right) + \beta U_t + k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \epsilon_t \\
&= k X_0 + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t. \tag{B.4}
\end{aligned}

The $\alpha$-dependent terms cancel exactly. Therefore for every $t \geq 0$,

Y_t \mid (U_{0:t}) \sim \mathcal{N}(k X_0 + \beta U_t, \sigma^2), \tag{B.5}

independent of $\alpha$.

Step 3 (joint law of the observation trajectory). Since $\epsilon_t$ are i.i.d.,

Y_{0:T-1} \mid U_{0:T-1} \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(k X_0 \mathbf{1}_T + \beta U_{0:T-1}, \sigma^2 I_T\right). \tag{B.6}

This distribution depends only on $(k X_0, \beta, \sigma^2, U_{0:T-1})$, not on $\alpha$. Hence

P_{M(k, \alpha_1, \beta, \sigma^2)}(Y_{0:T-1} \mid U_{0:T-1}) = P_{M(k, \alpha_2, \beta, \sigma^2)}(Y_{0:T-1} \mid U_{0:T-1}) \tag{B.7}

for all $\alpha_1, \alpha_2$ and all $U_{0:T-1}$.

Step 4 (target distribution). The target at time $T$ is

T(X_T) = X_T = X_0 - \alpha \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s, \tag{B.8}

a Dirac mass at $X_0 - \alpha \sum_s U_s$. Therefore

P_{M(k, \alpha_i)}(T(X_T) \mid U) = \delta_{X_0 - \alpha_i \sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s}. \tag{B.9}

Step 5 (Wasserstein separation). For Dirac measures at points $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$,

W_1(\delta_a, \delta_b) = |a - b|. \tag{B.10}

Hence

W_1^T\!\left(P_{M(k, \alpha_1)}(T(X_T)), P_{M(k, \alpha_2)}(T(X_T))\right) = |\alpha_1 - \alpha_2| \cdot \left|\sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s\right|. \tag{B.11}

Step 6 (gap). Since $M(k, \alpha_1, \beta, \sigma^2)$ and $M(k, \alpha_2, \beta, \sigma^2)$ are in $\mathcal{Q}$ and observationally equivalent,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \geq |\alpha_1 - \alpha_2| \cdot \left|\sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s\right|. \tag{B.12}

If $U_{0:T-1}$ is such that $\sum_s U_s \neq 0$, and $\alpha_1 \neq \alpha_2$, the gap is strictly positive. $\blacksquare$

B.2 Necessity of history-dependent observation

Proposition B.1. Suppose the observation function depends only on $(X_t, U_t)$:

Y_t = k X_t + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t. \tag{B.13}

Then the construction of Theorem A fails for $T \geq 2$: no $\alpha$-invariance can be achieved without an additional history-dependent term.

Proof. Under (B.13), substituting (B.3):

Y_t = k X_0 - k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t. \tag{B.14}

For $\alpha$-invariance at all $t \geq 0$, we would need

-k\alpha \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} U_s = 0 \qquad \forall t \geq 1, \tag{B.15}

which requires $\alpha U_s = 0$ for all $s$, contradicting $\alpha \neq 0$ and $U_s \neq 0$. Hence the family $\{Y_t\}$ is $\alpha$-dependent whenever $\sum_s U_s \neq 0$ for some $t$. $\blacksquare$

Remark B.1. The history dependence in (B.2) has a natural interpretation: cumulative intervention exposure contributes a direct additive term to the measurement. This is physically plausible in biological settings where the biomarker integrates exposure over time (e.g., cumulative drug metabolites, epigenetic marks accumulating over repeated treatments).

B.3 Bounded vs. unbounded gap

Proposition B.2. If $\alpha \in [\alpha_{\min}, \alpha_{\max}]$ with $\alpha_{\max} - \alpha_{\min} < \infty$ and $|U_t| \leq U_{\max}$, then

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \leq (\alpha_{\max} - \alpha_{\min}) \cdot T \cdot U_{\max}. \tag{B.16}

Proof. Immediate from (B.12) and $|\sum_{s=0}^{T-1} U_s| \leq T U_{\max}$. $\blacksquare$

Proposition B.3. If the admissible class permits $\alpha \to \infty$ or $|U_s| \to \infty$, then $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$ may be unbounded.

The rigorous statement of the unbounded-gap result therefore requires an explicit unboundedness assumption on $\mathcal{Q}$ or $\mathcal{U}$.

