Appendix C — Full Proof of Theorem C (Pathological Compensation)

C.1 Restatement

System:

x_{1,t+1} = x_{1,t} + u_{1,t}, \qquad x_{2,t+1} = x_{2,t} + u_{2,t}, \tag{C.1}

Y_t = x_{1,t} - x_{2,t} + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t, \qquad T(X) = x_1 + x_2. \tag{C.2}

C.2 Lie derivative computation

The drift vector field is $F(X, U) = (x_1 + u_1, x_2 + u_2)^\top$. The observation function is $h(X, U) = x_1 - x_2 + \beta U$.

Zeroth-order term. $L_F^0 h = h = x_1 - x_2 + \beta U$, so

dh = (1, -1, \beta) \quad \text{(differentiating in } (X, U)). \tag{C.3}

First-order term. The Lie derivative $L_F h = \langle dh, F \rangle$:

L_F h = (1, -1) \cdot (x_1 + u_1, x_2 + u_2) + \beta \dot{U} = (x_1 + u_1) - (x_2 + u_2) + \beta \dot{U}. \tag{C.4}

For discrete time, the analogue of the Lie derivative is $h \circ F$:

h(F(X, U), U') = (x_1 + u_1) - (x_2 + u_2) + \beta U' = x_1 - x_2 + u_1 - u_2 + \beta U'. \tag{C.5}

Higher-order terms. Iterating:

h(F^k(X, U_{0:k-1}), U_k) = x_1 - x_2 + \sum_{j=1}^{k} (u_{1,j-1} - u_{2,j-1}) + \beta U_k. \tag{C.6}

The state-dependent part is always $x_1 - x_2$, independent of $k$.

C.3 Kernel of the observability codistribution

For each $k \geq 0$, the differential with respect to $X$ is

\frac{\partial}{\partial X} h(F^k(X)) = (1, -1). \tag{C.7}

Therefore the observability codistribution is

\mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{(1, -1)^\top\}. \tag{C.8}

Its kernel is

\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{(1, 1)^\top\}. \tag{C.9}

C.4 Target direction

The target differential is

dT = (1, 1). \tag{C.10}

Since $(1, 1)^\top \in \ker \mathcal{O}_F(h)$ but $dT \cdot (1, 1)^\top = 2 \neq 0$,

\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) \not\subseteq \ker dT. \tag{C.11}

C.5 Independence of diagnostic input

The diagnostic input $v$ enters through $u_1, u_2$. The observable part of the output trajectory is

Y_t - \beta U_t = x_1 - x_2 + \epsilon_t, \tag{C.12}

which is independent of $u_1 + u_2$ and depends only on $x_1 - x_2$. No choice of $v$ can rotate the observability codistribution toward $(1, 1)^\top$ because the state dependence is fixed by (C.7). Hence for all $K, v$,

(1, 1)^\top \in \ker \mathscr{O}_K(v). \tag{C.13}

C.6 Implication for identification

Since $x_1 + x_2$ is unobservable for all inputs, two states $X = (x_1, x_2)$ and $X' = (x_1 + a, x_2 + a)$ produce identical observation distributions but different targets:

T(X) - T(X') = 2a. \tag{C.14}

Therefore $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \geq 2|a|$ whenever both $X$ and $X'$ are admissible. The direct channel $D_U h = \beta$ can be estimated exactly (from a fast diagnostic perturbation), but this does not reduce $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$. $\blacksquare$

C.7 Generalization

Proposition C.1. Let $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$ be the observability codistribution of a nonlinear system. If

\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) \not\subseteq \ker dT, \tag{C.15}

then for every diagnostic input class $\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{diag}}$,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{diag}}) > 0, \tag{C.16}

regardless of channel separation.

Proof. The unobservable direction $\delta X \in \ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) \setminus \ker dT$ satisfies $h(F^k(X + \delta X)) = h(F^k(X))$ for all $k$, but $T(X + \delta X) \neq T(X)$. Construct two models $M_1, M_2$ with identical observation law but latent states differing by $\delta X$. They are observationally equivalent, but $T$-inequivalent. $\blacksquare$

