Appendix D — Nonlinear Observability: Lie Derivatives, Codistributions, and Rank Conditions

D.1 Continuous-time observability

Consider $\dot{X} = F(X, U)$, $Y = h(X, U)$ with $F, h$ smooth. The Lie derivative of $h$ along $F$ is

L_F h(X, U) = \frac{\partial h}{\partial X}(X, U) \cdot F(X, U). \tag{D.1}

Higher-order Lie derivatives are defined recursively:

L_F^k h = L_F(L_F^{k-1} h) = \frac{\partial (L_F^{k-1} h)}{\partial X} \cdot F. \tag{D.2}

The observability codistribution is

\mathcal{O}_F(h) = \mathrm{span}\{dh, dL_F h, dL_F^2 h, \ldots\} \subseteq T^\ast \mathcal{X}. \tag{D.3}

D.2 Discrete-time observability

For discrete-time $X_{t+1} = F(X_t, U_t)$, the analogue is generated by the differentials of the observability maps

h_k(X, U_{0:k}) = h(F^k(X, U_{0:k-1}), U_k), \qquad k \geq 0. \tag{D.4}

The discrete-time observability matrix at horizon $K$ is

\mathcal{O}_K(X, U) = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial h_0}{\partial X}(X, U_0) \\ \frac{\partial h_1}{\partial X}(X, U_{0:1}) \\ \vdots \\ \frac{\partial h_{K-1}}{\partial X}(X, U_{0:K-1}) \end{bmatrix}. \tag{D.5}

D.3 Constant-rank assumption

Assumption (CR). On a neighborhood $K \subseteq \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U}$ of the nominal trajectory, the observability codistribution $\mathcal{O}_F(h)$ has constant rank $r \leq n$.

Under (CR), $\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h)$ is a smooth distribution of dimension $n - r$, and the Frobenius theorem guarantees local integrability.

D.4 Local weak observability

Definition D.1. The system is locally weakly observable at $X_0$ if there exists a neighborhood $V$ of $X_0$ such that for any $X \in V \setminus \{X_0\}$, there exists a horizon $K$ and an input sequence $U_{0:K}$ such that the corresponding output trajectories $h_k(X, U_{0:k})$ and $h_k(X_0, U_{0:k})$ differ for some $k \leq K$.

Theorem D.1 (Rank condition for local weak observability). Under (CR) and smoothness of $F, h$, the system is locally weakly observable at $X_0$ if and only if

\mathrm{rank}\, \mathcal{O}_F(h)(X_0) = n. \tag{D.6}

Proof. This is the Hermann–Krener rank condition. See Isidori, Nonlinear Control Systems, Ch. 1. $\blacksquare$

D.5 Target-relevant observability

Definition D.2. The target $T : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^q$ is target-relevant observable at $X_0$ if

\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h)(X_0) \subseteq \ker dT(X_0). \tag{D.7}

Lemma D.1. Under (CR), (D.7) holds on a neighborhood of $X_0$ iff there exist smooth functions $\varphi_1, \ldots, \varphi_q$ such that

T_i = \varphi_i(Y, \dot{Y}, \ldots, Y^{(K-1)}), \qquad i = 1, \ldots, q, \tag{D.8}

for some finite $K$.

Proof. If (D.7) holds, then each $dT_i$ is in the span of the observability codistribution, so $T_i$ is a smooth function of the observability coordinates. The converse follows from the chain rule. $\blacksquare$

