Appendix E — Variational System and Intervention-Induced Observability

E.1 Derivation of the variational system

Consider the nominal trajectory $X_t^\ast$ generated by $X_{t+1}^\ast = F(X_t^\ast, U_t^\ast)$ and a perturbed trajectory $X_t = X_t^\ast + \delta X_t$ generated by $X_{t+1} = F(X_t, U_t^\ast + \delta U_t)$.

A first-order Taylor expansion gives

\delta X_{t+1} = D_X F(X_t^\ast, U_t^\ast) \delta X_t + D_U F(X_t^\ast, U_t^\ast) \delta U_t + O(\|\delta X_t\|^2 + \|\delta U_t\|^2). \tag{E.1}

Define $A_t = D_X F(X_t^\ast, U_t^\ast)$ and $B_t = D_U F(X_t^\ast, U_t^\ast)$. The variational system is

\delta X_{t+1} = A_t \delta X_t + B_t \delta U_t. \tag{E.2}

E.2 Perturbation propagation

For a diagnostic perturbation $\delta U_t = \delta_t v$, the perturbation propagates as

\delta X_{t+k} = \Phi_{t+k, t} \delta X_t + \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \Phi_{t+k, t+j+1} B_{t+j} \delta_{t+j} v, \tag{E.3}

where $\Phi_{t+k, t}$ is the state-transition matrix of the variational system:

\Phi_{t+k, t} = A_{t+k-1} A_{t+k-2} \cdots A_t. \tag{E.4}

For a linear time-invariant system, $\Phi_{t+k, t} = A^k$.

E.3 Output perturbation

The output perturbation is

\delta Y_{t+k} = D_X h(X_{t+k}^\ast) \delta X_{t+k} + D_U h(X_{t+k}^\ast) \delta U_{t+k} + O(\|\delta X\|^2). \tag{E.5}

For a pure diagnostic experiment at $t = 0$ with $\delta U_0 = v$ and $\delta U_{t>0} = 0$:

\delta Y_k = D_X h \cdot A^{k-1} B v + O(\|v\|^2). \tag{E.6}

E.4 Intervention-induced observability matrix

Stacking (E.6) over $k = 1, \ldots, K$:

\mathscr{O}_K(v) = \begin{bmatrix} D_X h \cdot B v \\ D_X h \cdot A B v \\ \vdots \\ D_X h \cdot A^{K-1} B v \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} D_X h B \\ D_X h A B \\ \vdots \\ D_X h A^{K-1} B \end{bmatrix} v. \tag{E.7}

Define the intervention-induced observability matrix (without dependence on $v$) as

\mathscr{O}_K^B = \begin{bmatrix} D_X h B \\ D_X h A B \\ \vdots \\ D_X h A^{K-1} B \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{mK \times p}. \tag{E.8}

Then $\mathscr{O}_K(v) = \mathscr{O}_K^B v$.

E.5 Target-relevant excitation condition

The target is $T(X) = LX$. The condition for target identification is

\ker \mathscr{O}_K^B \subseteq \ker L. \tag{E.9}

Equivalently, the target direction $L$ must be in the row space of the intervention-induced observability matrix.

E.6 Interaction with state observability

Lemma E.1. If $T$ is observable from the state ($\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker L$) and if the diagnostic perturbation excites the full state space ($\mathrm{rank}\, \mathscr{O}_K^B = n$), then $T$ is identifiable.

Proof. If $\mathscr{O}_K^B$ has rank $n$, then $\ker \mathscr{O}_K^B = \{0\}$, so (E.9) holds trivially. $\blacksquare$

Remark E.1. The condition (E.9) is strictly weaker than full excitation: it only requires that the unexcited directions be target-neutral.

