Appendix G — Linear-Gaussian Specialization: Complete Proofs

G.1 Setup

X_{t+1} = AX_t + BU_t + \eta_t, \qquad \eta_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\eta), \tag{G.1}

Y_t = CX_t + DU_t + \epsilon_t, \qquad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\epsilon), \tag{G.2}

with $T(X) = LX$.

G.2 Observability matrix and target observability

The finite-horizon observability matrix is

\mathcal{O}_K = \begin{bmatrix} C \\ CA \\ \vdots \\ CA^{K-1} \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{mK \times n}. \tag{G.3}

Lemma G.1. The following are equivalent:

1. $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker L$.
2. There exists $M \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times mK}$ with $L = M \mathcal{O}_K$.
3. For every $X \in \mathbb{R}^n$, $LX$ is determined by $\mathcal{O}_K X$.

Proof. (1) $\Rightarrow$ (2): By the rank-nullity theorem, $L$ factors through $\mathcal{O}_K$ because $L$ vanishes on $\ker \mathcal{O}_K$. The factorization $M$ exists by the universal property of quotients. (2) $\Rightarrow$ (3): Immediate. (3) $\Rightarrow$ (1): If $\mathcal{O}_K X = 0$, then $LX$ must be the same for $X$ and $0$, so $LX = 0$. $\blacksquare$

G.3 Decomposition of the target

Under Lemma G.1,

LX_T = M \mathcal{O}_K X_T = M \begin{bmatrix} C X_T \\ CA X_T \\ \vdots \\ CA^{K-1} X_T \end{bmatrix}. \tag{G.4}

G.4 Expression of $CA^k X_T$ in terms of observations

Derivation. From (G.2) and (G.1):

Y_T = CX_T + DU_T + \epsilon_T \;\Rightarrow\; CX_T = Y_T - DU_T - \epsilon_T. \tag{G.5}

Y_{T-1} = CX_{T-1} + DU_{T-1} + \epsilon_{T-1} = C(A^{-1}(X_T - BU_{T-1} - \eta_{T-1})) + DU_{T-1} + \epsilon_{T-1}. \tag{G.6}

For general $k$:

CA^k X_T = Y_{T-k} - DU_{T-k} - \epsilon_{T-k} - \sum_{j=1}^{k-1} C A^{k-1-j} B U_{T-k+j} - (\text{noise terms in } \eta). \tag{G.7}

The noise terms in $\eta$ are not observable, but they are Gaussian with known covariance, and their contribution to $LX_T$ has bounded variance.

G.5 Cleaned observation

Define $\widetilde{Y}_t = Y_t - D U_t$. Then

\widetilde{Y}_t = CX_t + \epsilon_t. \tag{G.8}

Under the diagnostic protocol, the direct channel $D$ is estimated as $\widehat{D}_N$, and the cleaned observation is $\widehat{Y}_t = Y_t - \widehat{D}_N U_t$.

G.6 Consistency of $\widehat{D}_N$

Assumption (D1). There exists a diagnostic protocol under which $\widehat{D}_N \to D$ in probability.

A sufficient condition: a fast-timescale perturbation $\delta U_t^{\mathrm{diag}} = \delta_t v^\star$ with $\delta_t \to 0$ and $N \delta_t^2 \to \infty$. Under this protocol, the fast response isolates $D v^\star$, and the slow latent response is negligible at the fast timescale.

G.7 Main result

Theorem G.1 (linear-Gaussian recovery). Under (D1) and $\ker \mathcal{O}_K \subseteq \ker L$,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \to 0 \quad \text{in probability as } N \to \infty. \tag{G.9}

Proof. Let $M_i, M_j \in \mathcal{Q}_N$ be observationally equivalent after channel estimation. Then

P_{M_i}(\widehat{Y}_{0:N-1} \mid U) = P_{M_j}(\widehat{Y}_{0:N-1} \mid U). \tag{G.10}

For Gaussian linear systems, equality of observation laws is equivalent to equality of the mean vectors and covariance matrices:

C_i \mathbb{E}[X_t] + D_i U_t = C_j \mathbb{E}[X_t] + D_j U_t, \qquad C_i \Sigma_t C_i^\top + \Sigma_\epsilon = C_j \Sigma_t C_j^\top + \Sigma_\epsilon. \tag{G.11}

Under (D1), $D_i = D_j = D$. Then

C_i \mathbb{E}[X_t] = C_j \mathbb{E}[X_t], \qquad C_i \Sigma_t C_i^\top = C_j \Sigma_t C_j^\top. \tag{G.12}

The target distributions are

L_i \mathbb{E}[X_T] = M_i \mathcal{O}_{K,i} \mathbb{E}[X_T], \qquad L_j \mathbb{E}[X_T] = M_j \mathcal{O}_{K,j} \mathbb{E}[X_T]. \tag{G.13}

Under the observability condition, $\mathcal{O}_{K,i} \mathbb{E}[X_T]$ determines $L_i \mathbb{E}[X_T]$, and similarly for $j$. Since (G.12) implies $\mathcal{O}_{K,i} \mathbb{E}[X_T] = \mathcal{O}_{K,j} \mathbb{E}[X_T]$ for the observable components, we get

L_i \mathbb{E}[X_T] = L_j \mathbb{E}[X_T]. \tag{G.14}

Analogously for the covariance. Hence the target distributions coincide. $\blacksquare$

G.8 Unknown dynamic parameters

Assumption (D2). The parameter vector $\vartheta = \mathrm{vec}(A, B, C, D)$ is identifiable from the observation law, i.e., the Fisher information matrix

\mathcal{I}(\vartheta) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[\nabla_\vartheta \log p_\vartheta(Y_{0:N} \mid U) \nabla_\vartheta \log p_\vartheta(Y_{0:N} \mid U)^\top\right] \tag{G.15}

is non-singular.

Under (D2) and standard regularity, $\widehat{\vartheta}_N \to \vartheta^\star$ in probability, and the argument of Theorem G.1 extends.

Remark G.1. (D2) is not automatically satisfied under PE alone. It requires that the diagnostic protocol makes all parameters identifiable, which is a stronger condition than excitation of the input directions.

