Appendix H — Derivation of the Finite-Sample Bound

H.1 Setup and regularity

Assumption (H1). The model class $\Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ is compact, and $M_\vartheta$ satisfies:

1. The log-likelihood $\ell_N(\vartheta) = \log p_\vartheta(\mathcal{D}_N)$ is twice continuously differentiable in $\vartheta$.
2. The Fisher information $I_1(\vartheta)$ is positive definite uniformly on $\Theta$.
3. The MLE $\hat{\vartheta}_N$ exists and is consistent.

H.2 Wilks' theorem

Theorem H.1 (Wilks). Under (H1) and the correct specification $M_{\vartheta^\star} \in \mathcal{M}$,

2\!\left(\ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) - \ell_N(\vartheta^\star)\right) \xrightarrow{d} \chi^2_d. \tag{H.1}

Proof. Standard; see van der Vaart, Asymptotic Statistics, Ch. 16. $\blacksquare$

H.3 Confidence set radius

Define

\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta) = \left\{\vartheta : 2(\ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) - \ell_N(\vartheta)) \leq \chi^2_d(1-\delta)\right\}. \tag{H.2}

Lemma H.1. Under (H1), for $\vartheta \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$,

(\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N)^\top J_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) (\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N) \leq \chi^2_d(1-\delta) + o_P(1). \tag{H.3}

Proof. Taylor expansion of $\ell_N$ around $\hat{\vartheta}_N$:

\ell_N(\vartheta) = \ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) + \nabla \ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N)^\top (\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N) - \tfrac{1}{2}(\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N)^\top J_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) (\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N) + R_N. \tag{H.4}

Since $\nabla \ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) = 0$ and $R_N = o_P(\|\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N\|^2)$,

2(\ell_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N) - \ell_N(\vartheta)) = (\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N)^\top J_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N)(\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N) + o_P(1). \tag{H.5}

The result follows. $\blacksquare$

H.4 Fisher metric radius

Lemma H.2. Under (H1), $J_N(\hat{\vartheta}_N)/N \to I_1(\vartheta^\star)$ in probability. Therefore

\|\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N\|_{I_1} \leq \sqrt{\frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N}} + o_P(1) \tag{H.6}

uniformly for $\vartheta \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$.

Proof. Since $J_N \succeq N \lambda_{\min}(I_1) I_d + o_P(N)$,

\|\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N\|_{I_1}^2 \leq \frac{(\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N)^\top J_N (\vartheta - \hat{\vartheta}_N)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_1)} \leq \frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_1)} + o_P(1). \tag{H.7}

The uniformity in $\vartheta$ follows from (H1) and the compactness of $\Theta$. $\blacksquare$

H.5 Lipschitz property of the target map

Assumption (H2). The map $g : \Theta \to \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^q)$, $g(\vartheta) = P_{M_\vartheta}(T(X_T) \mid U)$, is Lipschitz with respect to the Fisher metric on $\Theta$ and the target-pushforward Wasserstein-1 metric:

W_1^T(g(\vartheta_1), g(\vartheta_2)) \leq L_T \|\vartheta_1 - \vartheta_2\|_{I_1}. \tag{H.8}

Lemma H.3. If $\ker \mathcal{O}_F(h) \subseteq \ker dT$ holds uniformly on the admissible set, then $L_T < \infty$.

Proof. Under the target-relevant observability condition, the target $T$ is a smooth function of the observable outputs and inputs, with bounded gradient. The Lipschitz constant is the supremum of the gradient norm over the admissible set, finite by compactness. $\blacksquare$

H.6 Main finite-sample bound

Theorem H.2 (finite-sample bound). Under (H1) and (H2), for any $\delta \in (0,1)$,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \leq 2 L_T \sqrt{\frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_1)}} + o_P\!\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\right). \tag{H.9}

Proof. For any $\vartheta_1, \vartheta_2 \in \mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$,

\begin{aligned}
W_1^T(g(\vartheta_1), g(\vartheta_2)) &\leq W_1^T(g(\vartheta_1), g(\hat{\vartheta}_N)) + W_1^T(g(\hat{\vartheta}_N), g(\vartheta_2)) \\
&\leq L_T \left(\|\vartheta_1 - \hat{\vartheta}_N\|_{I_1} + \|\vartheta_2 - \hat{\vartheta}_N\|_{I_1}\right) \\
&\leq 2 L_T \sqrt{\frac{\chi^2_d(1-\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_1)}} + o_P(1/\sqrt{N}). \tag{H.10}
\end{aligned}

Taking the supremum over observationally indistinguishable pairs in $\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)$ gives the bound. $\blacksquare$

H.7 Target-relevant refinement

Definition H.1. The target Fisher information is

I_T(\vartheta) = \left[\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial \vartheta}\right) I_1(\vartheta)^{-1} \left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial \vartheta}\right)^\top\right]^{-1} \in \mathbb{R}^{q \times q}. \tag{H.11}

Theorem H.3 (refined bound). Under (H1), (H2), and positive-definiteness of $I_T$,

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \lesssim \sqrt{\frac{q + \log(1/\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_T)}}. \tag{H.12}

Proof sketch. The likelihood-ratio confidence set in the $q$-dimensional target space is approximately a $\chi^2_q$ ellipsoid with radius $\sqrt{\chi^2_q(1-\delta)/N}$ in the $I_T$ metric. The Lipschitz constant $L_T$ is absorbed into the definition of $I_T$ via the chain rule. $\blacksquare$

H.8 Nonparametric extension

Assumption (H3). $\Theta$ is a Hölder ball $\mathcal{H}^\beta(\mathcal{X})$ of smoothness $\beta$ and radius $R$, with metric entropy

\log \mathcal{N}(\epsilon, \Theta, \|\cdot\|_\infty) \lesssim \epsilon^{-d/\beta}. \tag{H.13}

Theorem H.4 (nonparametric rate). Under (H1), (H2), and (H3),

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N(\delta)) \lesssim L_T \left(\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{N}\right)^{\frac{\beta}{2\beta + d}} + o_P(N^{-\beta/(2\beta+d)}). \tag{H.14}

Proof sketch. The confidence set radius under (H3) is $\asymp (\log(1/\delta)/N)^{\beta/(2\beta+d)}$ by standard entropy bounds (see van de Geer, Empirical Processes in M-Estimation). The Lipschitz propagation gives the result. $\blacksquare$

H.9 Correctness of the finite-sample bound

Remark H.1 (locality). The bound is stated with $L_T$ and $I_T$ evaluated pointwise. Uniform control over $\Theta$ requires uniform positivity of $I_T$ and uniform boundedness of $L_T$, which hold under (H1)–(H2) on compact $\Theta$.

Remark H.2 (misspecification). If $M^\star \notin \mathcal{Q}$, the confidence set may not contain $M^\star$. The bound then measures the width of $\mathcal{Q}_N$ rather than the distance from truth. Misspecification requires a separate analysis (Appendix I).

Remark H.3 (looseness). The factor of 2 and the $\chi^2$ approximation are not tight. A Bernstein–von Mises refinement would replace the $\chi^2$ quantile with a Gaussian quantile, gaining a constant factor but not changing the rate.

