Appendix I — Misspecification and Model-Class Sensitivity

I.1 Setup

Let $M^\star$ be the true data-generating process. Define the projection of $M^\star$ onto $\mathcal{Q}$ by

M^\ast_\mathcal{Q} = \arg\min_{M \in \mathcal{Q}} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(P_{M^\star}(Y_{0:N} \mid U) \,\|\, P_M(Y_{0:N} \mid U)\right). \tag{I.1}

I.2 Bias-variance decomposition

Theorem I.1. Under regularity, the target gap admits the decomposition

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}_N) \leq \underbrace{\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}^{\mathrm{bias}}(\mathcal{Q})}_{\text{model-class bias}} + \underbrace{\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}^{\mathrm{var}}(\mathcal{Q}_N)}_{\text{finite-sample variance}}. \tag{I.2}

Proof. Triangle inequality on the Wasserstein metric:

W_1^T(P_{M_i}(T), P_{M_j}(T)) \leq W_1^T(P_{M_i}(T), P_{M^\ast_\mathcal{Q}}(T)) + W_1^T(P_{M^\ast_\mathcal{Q}}(T), P_{M_j}(T)). \tag{I.3}

The first term is bounded by the model-class bias, the second by the finite-sample variance. $\blacksquare$

I.3 Model-class bias

Definition I.1. The model-class bias for the target is

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}^{\mathrm{bias}}(\mathcal{Q}) = \sup_{M \in \mathcal{Q}} W_1^T(P_M(T(X_T)), P_{M^\star}(T(X_T))). \tag{I.4}

Proposition I.1. If $M^\star \in \mathcal{Q}$, then $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}^{\mathrm{bias}} = 0$. If $M^\star \notin \mathcal{Q}$, then $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}^{\mathrm{bias}} > 0$ unless the projection happens to match the target law exactly.

I.4 Sensitivity to model-class enlargement

Proposition I.2. Let $\mathcal{Q} \subseteq \mathcal{Q}'$ be admissible model classes. Then

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}') \geq \Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}(\mathcal{Q}). \tag{I.5}

Proof. The supremum in (15) is over a larger set when the class is enlarged. $\blacksquare$

Remark I.1. Model-class enlargement can only increase the gap. This is why identification claims must specify $\mathcal{Q}$ explicitly: a broader class may render the target non-identifiable even when it is identifiable in a narrower class.

I.5 Robust PCI

Replace the point-mass model class with an ambiguity set $\mathcal{P}$ over transition and observation kernels. Define

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}^{\mathrm{robust}}(\mathcal{P}) = \sup_{\substack{P_i, P_j \in \mathcal{P} \\ P_i \sim_{\mathcal{D},U} P_j}} W_1^T(P_i(T(X_T)), P_j(T(X_T))). \tag{I.6}

The robust gap is larger than the parametric gap and provides a distributionally robust certificate of non-identifiability.

