Appendix J — Numerical Illustration of the Two-State Counterexample

J.1 Setup

Consider the system from Section 7 with $x_{1,0} = 1$, $x_{2,0} = 0$, $\beta = 1$, $\epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.01)$. We simulate two trajectories:

· Trajectory A: $u_{1,t} = 0$, $u_{2,t} = 0$.
· Trajectory B: $u_{1,t} = 1$, $u_{2,t} = 1$.

Both trajectories produce identical observations $Y_t = x_1 - x_2 + \beta U_t + \epsilon_t = 1 + \epsilon_t$, but different targets $T_A = 1$, $T_B = 3$.

J.2 Diagnostic test

Apply a diagnostic perturbation $v = (1, -1)^\top$ with magnitude $\delta = 0.1$. The observation changes by

\Delta Y_t = (1, -1) \cdot (0.1, -0.1) + \beta \cdot 0.1 = 0 + 0.1 = 0.1. \tag{J.1}

This change is entirely due to the direct channel. The latent state changes by $(0.1, -0.1)$, which is invisible to $Y$ but changes $T$ by $0$. Apply $v = (1, 1)^\top$ with magnitude $\delta = 0.1$. The observation changes by

\Delta Y_t = (1, -1) \cdot (0.1, 0.1) + \beta \cdot 0.1 = 0 + 0.1 = 0.1. \tag{J.2}

The latent state changes by $(0.1, 0.1)$, which changes $T$ by $0.2$ but is invisible to $Y$. This demonstrates numerically that the target direction is unobservable regardless of the diagnostic input.

J.3 Estimation of $\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}}$

For $N = 1000$ samples under the diagnostic protocol, the finite-sample bound (Theorem H.3) gives

\Gamma_{\mathrm{PC}} \lesssim \sqrt{\frac{q + \log(1/\delta)}{N \lambda_{\min}(I_T)}}. \tag{J.3}

However, in this example $I_T$ is singular (the target direction is unobservable), so $\lambda_{\min}(I_T) = 0$, and the bound is vacuous. This is the correct behavior: the structural condition fails, so no finite-sample bound applies.

